#open-question(title: [Can the Pattern of Nulls Across the Four Drugs Constrain Mechanism by Inverse Negative Control?])[
This is a research-method proposal, not a claim with direct supporting evidence. The core idea is inverse negative control: if each drug probes one mediator arm, then the *pattern of which drugs do nothing* (the "null fingerprint") could constrain which arm is — and is not — driving a patient's symptoms. Two methodologically relevant but indirect anchors exist. N-of-1 trials show that individual response heterogeneity can coexist with a null group mean, so single-drug group trials miss responder subsets and a per-patient fingerprint is the only way to capture them @Ortiz2026Nof1Trials. Histamine-receptor blockade produces a clean null on exercise domain boundary and time-to-task-failure in healthy adults, demonstrating that histamine blockade can be genuinely inert for a given outcome @Abbotts2025HistamineExercise. There is no MCAS-specific non-response literature, and no study has tested whether a four-arm null pattern maps onto mechanism — the method is unproven.

This is an open question; no certainty is assigned. The N-of-1 and null-result anchors are real but come from different fields; the fingerprint-to-mechanism inference is untested.

*Falsifiable prediction:* In MCAS/POTS patients, a four-arm N-of-1 design shows that the null-response pattern across H1/PAF, H2, CysLT1, and cholinergic arms is stable within a patient over repeated cycles and is mechanistically informative (for example a null on H2 but a response to CysLT1 predicts elevated CysLT-dependent, H2-independent activation); if the null pattern is unstable across cycles or uninformative about mechanism, inverse negative control fails as a diagnostic tool.

*Consequence:* If it works, non-response — usually discarded as "the drug did not help" — would become diagnostic information, giving clinicians a cheap, safe way to localise mechanism in a disease with no validated biomarker. Until then it must be treated strictly as a methods proposal with no direct evidence.
] <oq:non-response-pharmacodiagnostic-fingerprint>
